Because both $P^{-b_1}$ and $1/P^{-b_1}$ are in $\mathcal L(t^\prime)$, we must have $P^{-b_1}=\lambda A_{1;t^\prime}^{c_1}\cdots A_{n;t^\prime}^{c_n}$ for some invertible element $\lambda$ in $\mathbb{KP}$ and $c_1,\ldots,c_n\in\mathbb Z$. Since $P$ is a polynomial in $\mathbb{KP}[A_{2;t^\prime},\ldots,A_{n;t^\prime}]$ with $A_{i;t^\prime}\nmid P$ for any $i$, we get that $c_1=\ldots=c_n=0$ and $P^{-b_1}=\lambda$ is invertible in $\mathbb{KP}$.  By our assumption in Subsection \ref{rmkexbi}, we know that  $P^{-b_1}=\lambda$ is not invertible in $\mathbb{KP}$. This concludes a contradiction. So we must have $b_1,\ldots,b_n\geq 0$.

